I want to build a "programmable CLI" that can be self configured for any use. A universal CLI. I have a transcript of a prior LLM conversation I'd like to share for context:
I'd like to write a new CLI system that resembles the approach that VYOS uses, with edit,top,up,commit etc. What language is this coded in?

VyOS relies on a combination of C, Python 3, XML, and OCaml depending on the specific layer of the system:

Interactive CLI Shell (vbash): Written in C. It is a modified fork of GNU Bash patched to manage stateful interactive navigation (edit, top, up), dynamic prompt updates, and context-aware tab completion via underlying C/C++ helper utilities (cli-shell-api).

Backend Configuration Engine (vyos-1x): Written in Python 3. Modern VyOS uses Python to parse configuration tree nodes, run validation checks (verify), render daemon configuration files using Jinja2 templates, and trigger service restarts during a commit.  
GitHub

Command Definitions: Defined declaratively in XML files structured around a RelaxNG schema. The command structure, parameter validation rules, and help strings are decoupled from backend code and defined as a tree of XML nodes.  
VyOS Documentation

Next-Generation Configuration Core (VyConf): Written in OCaml. VyOS developed VyConf as a centralized configuration daemon (vyconfd) to handle tree processing, transactional commits, diff generation, and rollback mechanisms.  
GitHub
+ 1

Legacy Code (Vyatta / Early VyOS): The original Vyatta architecture relied heavily on Perl wrappers and Bash scripts attached to file-system directory trees (node.def files) to determine CLI paths.  
Reddit

Recommended Tech Stack for a Custom Implementation
If you want to build a similar hierarchical CLI with candidate/running configuration states (set, edit, top, up, commit, rollback) today:

Python: Use prompt_toolkit for the interactive shell (it natively supports nested context prompts, dynamic auto-completion, keybindings, and syntax highlighting) paired with an in-memory tree data structure for candidate configuration tracking.

Go: Ideal for compiling down to a single binary. Combine chzyer/readline or c-bata/go-prompt with a custom node path tracker.

Rust: Recommended for system-level safety and speed. Use rustyline for the line-editing REPL interface alongside custom transactional data structures.

I was thinking building a streamlined version of this in Rust. How would that work?

A streamlined VyOS-style CLI in Rust requires five core components: an interactive REPL library, a hierarchical tree data structure, session path tracking, command-line mutation helpers, and a transaction/commit engine.

1. The Configuration Tree Structure
VyOS treats configurations as nested trees. A BTreeMap is optimal because it preserves key ordering for canonical output and predictable diffing.

Rust
use std::collections::BTreeMap;

#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq)]
pub enum ConfigNode {
    /// A leaf node holding a concrete value (e.g., "192.168.1.1/24")
    Value(String),
    /// A multi-value leaf node (e.g., list of DNS servers)
    Tag(Vec<String>),
    /// A nested container containing child nodes
    Container(BTreeMap<String, ConfigNode>),
}

impl ConfigNode {
    pub fn new_container() -> Self {
        ConfigNode::Container(BTreeMap::new())
    }
}
2. Session Context & Path Navigation
The session keeps track of where the user is currently focused (working_path), along with two separate configuration trees: running (active system state) and candidate (uncommitted draft).

Rust
pub struct CliSession {
    pub working_path: Vec<String>,
    pub candidate: ConfigNode,
    pub running: ConfigNode,
}

impl CliSession {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            working_path: Vec::new(),
            candidate: ConfigNode::new_container(),
            running: ConfigNode::new_container(),
        }
    }

    /// Resolves full target path based on current context
    pub fn resolve_path(&self, input_path: &[String]) -> Vec<String> {
        let mut full_path = self.working_path.clone();
        full_path.extend_from_slice(input_path);
        full_path
    }

    /// Handles navigation commands: edit, up, top
    pub fn navigate(&mut self, cmd: &str, args: &[String]) {
        match cmd {
            "edit" => {
                self.working_path.extend_from_slice(args);
            }
            "up" => {
                if !self.working_path.is_empty() {
                    self.working_path.pop();
                }
            }
            "top" => {
                self.working_path.clear();
            }
            _ => {}
        }
    }

    /// Generates dynamic shell prompt: `[interfaces ethernet eth0]# `
    pub fn prompt(&self) -> String {
        if self.working_path.is_empty() {
            "# ".to_string()
        } else {
            format!("[{}]# ", self.working_path.join(" "))
        }
    }
}
3. Tree Mutations (set, delete, show)
When a user executes set ip address 10.0.0.1/24 inside [interfaces ethernet eth0], the path resolves relative to working_path.

Rust
impl ConfigNode {
    pub fn set_path(&mut self, path: &[String], value: String) {
        if path.is_empty() {
            return;
        }

        if let ConfigNode::Container(map) = self {
            if path.len() == 1 {
                map.insert(path[0].clone(), ConfigNode::Value(value));
            } else {
                let entry = map
                    .entry(path[0].clone())
                    .or_insert_with(ConfigNode::new_container);
                entry.set_path(&path[1..], value);
            }
        }
    }
}
4. The Commit & Diff Engine
The transaction lifecycle compares the candidate tree with the running tree, generates delta events, and applies changes atomically.

Diff Calculation: Recursive comparison between running and candidate yielding a stream of Added(path, val), Removed(path), or Modified(path, old, new).

Validation Phase: Pre-commit hooks run validation logic across the diff (e.g., verifying IP address syntax or port availability).

Apply Phase: System scripts apply changes (e.g., issuing ip addr add commands via Linux netlink).

Sync: Upon successful apply, running = candidate.clone().

Discard: Executing discard simply resets candidate = running.clone().

5. Interactive REPL Frontend
Use the reedline crate (developed for Nushell) or rustyline for the line editor. They provide line editing, command history, and dynamic auto-completion.

Rust
use reedline::{DefaultPrompt, Reedline, Signal};

fn main() {
    let mut line_editor = Reedline::create();
    let mut session = CliSession::new();

    loop {
        let prompt_str = session.prompt();
        let prompt = DefaultPrompt::new(
            reedline::DefaultPromptConfig::custom(prompt_str, "".to_string())
        );

        match line_editor.read_line(&prompt) {
            Ok(Signal::Success(line)) => {
                let parts: Vec<String> = line.split_whitespace().map(String::from).collect();
                if parts.is_empty() { continue; }

                match parts[0].as_str() {
                    "edit" | "up" | "top" => session.navigate(&parts[0], &parts[1..]),
                    "set" => {
                        let full_path = session.resolve_path(&parts[1..parts.len()-1]);
                        let val = parts.last().unwrap().clone();
                        session.candidate.set_path(&full_path, val);
                    }
                    "commit" => {
                        // Perform commit diff and apply logic
                        session.running = session.candidate.clone();
                    }
                    "exit" => break,
                    _ => {}
                }
            }
            Ok(Signal::CtrlC) | Ok(Signal::CtrlD) => break,
            Err(err) => {
                eprintln!("Error: {:?}", err);
                break;
            }
        }
    }
}
Recommended Rust Crates
reedline: Line editing, prompt rendering, custom tab completion hooks, and keybindings.

rtnetlink: Async netlink crate if your CLI manages Linux networking interfaces natively.

serde & ron or toml: Serializing and deserializing the candidate tree to disk configs.

nom or pest: Parsing command strings or custom schema definitions if command syntax expands beyond basic whitespace splits.

My idea is to create a CLI that actually doesn't do any system interaction or network, but is instead a context aware JSON object authoring tool, allowing for a user to enter complex tree structures and edit them. A universal JSON interaction CLI

Mapping the VyOS CLI paradigm to JSON authoring works cleanly because serde_json::Value already models nested JSON structures natively as an enum of primitives, arrays, and key-value maps.

Data Model & Navigation Context
Instead of building a custom tree, use serde_json::Value directly. Context path navigation tracks array indices and object keys using a custom PathSegment enum.

Rust
use serde_json::{json, Map, Value};

#[derive(Clone, Debug)]
pub enum PathSegment {
    Key(String),
    Index(usize),
}

pub struct JsonAuthorSession {
    pub current_path: Vec<PathSegment>,
    pub candidate: Value,
    pub running: Value,
}

impl JsonAuthorSession {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            current_path: Vec::new(),
            candidate: Value::Object(Map::new()),
            running: Value::Object(Map::new()),
        }
    }

    /// Renders dynamic prompt: `[users 0 address]# `
    pub fn prompt(&self) -> String {
        if self.current_path.is_empty() {
            "/# ".to_string()
        } else {
            let path_str = self
                .current_path
                .iter()
                .map(|p| match p {
                    PathSegment::Key(k) => k.clone(),
                    PathSegment::Index(i) => i.to_string(),
                })
                .collect::<Vec<_>>()
                .join("/");
            format!("[{}]# ", path_str)
        }
    }
}
Tree Traversal & Mutation Engine
The mutation engine navigates the serde_json::Value recursively to modify, insert, or create sub-objects and arrays on demand.

Rust
impl JsonAuthorSession {
    /// Evaluates user text into typed JSON primitives or structures
    fn parse_value(input: &str) -> Value {
        if input == "{}" || input == "object" {
            Value::Object(Map::new())
        } else if input == "[]" || input == "array" {
            Value::Array(Vec::new())
        } else if let Ok(b) = input.parse::<bool>() {
            Value::Bool(b)
        } else if let Ok(n) = input.parse::<i64>() {
            Value::Number(n.into())
        } else if let Ok(f) = input.parse::<f64>() {
            serde_json::Number::from_f64(f)
                .map(Value::Number)
                .unwrap_or_else(|| Value::String(input.to_string()))
        } else {
            // Trim quotes if manually supplied
            Value::String(input.trim_matches('"').to_string())
        }
    }

    /// Mutates the candidate JSON at a target pointer location
    pub fn set(&mut self, path_args: &[String], value_str: &str) -> Result<(), String> {
        let mut full_path = self.current_path.clone();
        for arg in path_args {
            if let Ok(idx) = arg.parse::<usize>() {
                full_path.push(PathSegment::Index(idx));
            } else {
                full_path.push(PathSegment::Key(arg.clone()));
            }
        }

        if full_path.is_empty() {
            return Err("Cannot set root directly".into());
        }

        let val = Self::parse_value(value_str);
        Self::set_recursive(&mut self.candidate, &full_path, val)
    }

    fn set_recursive(current: &mut Value, path: &[PathSegment], val: Value) -> Result<(), String> {
        if path.len() == 1 {
            match (&path[0], current) {
                (PathSegment::Key(k), Value::Object(map)) => {
                    map.insert(k.clone(), val);
                    Ok(())
                }
                (PathSegment::Index(i), Value::Array(arr)) => {
                    if *i < arr.len() {
                        arr[*i] = val;
                    } else if *i == arr.len() {
                        arr.push(val);
                    } else {
                        return Err(format!("Index {} out of bounds for array length {}", i, arr.len()));
                    }
                    Ok(())
                }
                _ => Err("Path mismatch with current JSON node structure".into()),
            }
        } else {
            match (&path[0], current) {
                (PathSegment::Key(k), Value::Object(map)) => {
                    let next = map.entry(k.clone()).or_insert_with(|| json!({}));
                    Self::set_recursive(next, &path[1..], val)
                }
                (PathSegment::Index(i), Value::Array(arr)) => {
                    if let Some(next) = arr.get_mut(*i) {
                        Self::set_recursive(next, &path[1..], val)
                    } else {
                        Err(format!("Index {} not found", i))
                    }
                }
                _ => Err("Invalid path trajectory".into()),
            }
        }
    }
}
Key Capabilities for JSON Authoring
1. Type-Informed Context Auto-Completion
By using reedline's Completer trait, you query the JSON node at current_path. If it is an object, auto-complete return keys (keys()). If it is an array, tab completion suggests valid array indices (0..len).

2. Dynamic Type Instantiation
To build nested objects interactively without inline JSON syntax, allow path creation commands:

set server object (Creates {"server": {}})

edit server (Moves prompt context into [server]# )

set ports array (Creates {"server": {"ports": []}})

edit ports (Moves prompt context into [server ports]# )

set 0 8080 (Appends 8080 to array)

3. Interactive Navigation Grammar

Command	Action	Example Transformation
edit <key>	Step into object field or array index	/# edit database → [database]#
up	Move up one nesting level	[db credentials]# up → [db]#
top	Return to JSON root	[a b c]# top → /#
set <key> <val>	Write string, number, bool, array, or object	set host "localhost"
delete <key>	Remove object key or array index	delete password
show	Print current node (or full tree if at root)	Displays colorized JSON or YAML representation
commit	Validate JSON schema (optional) & copy candidate to running	Syncs draft to memory
save <file.json>	Write candidate tree to disk	Persists document to file
4. JSON Schema Validation on Commit
Integrate the jsonschema crate. Load an optional schema at shell startup. When executing commit, validate candidate JSON against the schema. If validation fails, halt the commit and report the field path error back to the user without discarding candidate state.

What value might a tool like this have, and what are some ways it can be used to prove agentic interaction with JSON?

Value Proposition of a State-Aware JSON CLI
Syntax-Error Elimination: Editing raw JSON directly in text editors often leads to broken brackets, missing commas, or unescaped quotes. A dedicated shell guarantees structural validity on every command.

Contextual Focus in Deep Trees: In multi-thousand-line documents (such as OpenAPI specs, Kubernetes manifests, or CloudFormation templates), working directly at the root creates cognitive overhead. Stepping into a path (edit networking/subnets/0) acts as a cd for data, isolating modifications to local context.

Transactional Staging & Safety: The candidate-vs-running pattern provides a safety net. Users draft changes, run a show diff to verify deltas across complex nested fields, and validate against a JSON Schema upon commit before writing to disk.

Terminal-Native Workflows: Provides a fast, lightweight interface for remote SSH administration, headless server configuration, and shell-scripted configuration building without GUI dependencies.

Proving and Benchmarking Agentic Interaction with JSON
An interactive CLI changes how autonomous AI agents manipulate structured data, moving from whole-file generation to step-by-step tree navigation and state mutation.

1. Token-Efficient State Manipulation
Generating a 3,000-line JSON document to change a single nested string consumes thousands of tokens and increases latency. Interacting via a stateful CLI reduces the agent's output to concise commands (edit spec/template, set replicas 3, commit), cutting context consumption by over 95%.

2. Stateful Self-Correction Loops
When an agent edits a file using standard code execution, schema errors require re-parsing the entire output. In a CLI session:

The agent executes commit.

The tool rejects the commit with a pinpointed error (Validation Error at /database/port: expected integer, got string).

The agent issues set port 5432 and re-commits within the same session history without destroying surrounding context.

3. Evaluating Structural & Spatial Reasoning
You can benchmark an LLM's understanding of nested structures by evaluating command efficiency:

Path Awareness: Does the agent recognize it is currently at [cluster/nodes/0] and execute set status "ready" rather than re-typing the absolute path set cluster/nodes/0/status "ready"?

Minimal Traversal: Does the agent use up efficiently to navigate sibling nodes, or does it repeatedly reset context with top?

4. Decoupling Semantic Intent from Syntax Rendering
By acting as an abstraction layer, the CLI forces the agent to focus purely on logical intent (what key to update to what value) while offloading JSON formatting, type coercion, and schema compliance checks to the underlying binary.

5. Deterministic Auditing & Step Validation
Standard LLM text outputs make tracking incremental changes difficult. A CLI produces a clean, linear command trace that maps directly to state transitions, simplifying human oversight, debugging, and step-by-step agent evaluation.

Why would I consider doing this in Rust vs Go for this tool?

Rust is the stronger choice for a state-aware JSON CLI because algebraic sum types eliminate runtime type-assertion bugs when traversing dynamic JSON trees, and modern REPL crates like reedline provide richer context-aware completion than Go's interactive prompt libraries.

Feature / Criteria	Rust (serde_json + reedline)	Go (encoding/json + go-prompt)
JSON Tree Representation	Native Enums (serde_json::Value): Strictly typed variants (Object, Array, String, etc.) handled safely via pattern matching.	Interface Casting (map[string]any): Requires repetitive type assertions (v.(map[string]any)), risking runtime panics on invalid paths.
REPL & Terminal Engine	reedline: Modern, actively maintained (powers Nushell) with native support for dynamic prompts, syntax highlighting, and inline completion.	go-prompt / readline: Workable, but key libraries are mostly unmaintained or require extensive custom state plumbing for dynamic prompts.
Navigation & Tree Safety	Exhaustive Matching: The compiler forces handling of every JSON variant during recursive path operations (set, edit, up).	Manual Type Guarding: Requires verbose switch v := val.(type) blocks for every traversal step.
Execution Performance	Zero GC / Fast Startup: Instant sub-millisecond execution and minimal binary size without garbage collection pauses.	Low Overhead: Fast startup and simple deployment, though GC triggers on heavy recursive tree operations.
Key Technical Advantages of Rust for This Specific Tool
Pattern Matching Over Type Assertions: Modifying heterogenous JSON nodes (e.g., navigating from an Object key into an Array index) requires continuous checking of the current node type. Rust's match statements guarantee that every edge case (out-of-bounds array indices, missing object keys, scalar-to-container type conversions) is handled explicitly at compile time.

Superior REPL Ecosystem: Building an interactive CLI like VyOS requires live path completion (tabbing through child keys of the current JSON node) and contextual prompt formatting ([users 0 address]#). The reedline crate natively supports custom completion hooks that query live data structures during keystrokes. In Go, implementing context-sensitive tab completion requires writing lower-level state logic around standard terminal libraries.

Schema Validation & Serde: Rust's serde ecosystem and crates like jsonschema allow effortless, zero-copy serialization and instant validation against schemas before executing a candidate commit. Go's reflection-heavy encoding/json is simpler to start with, but becomes unwieldy when constantly mutating partially formed candidate ASTs.

When Go Might Still Be Preferred
Go is worth considering if team familiarity favors it, or if rapid implementation of simple network/HTTP sidecars is required. However, for a pure terminal TUI/REPL operating on nested hierarchical data structures, Rust's type system provides significantly higher reliability and a better interactive developer experience.

Got it, if there's no legacy, incumbancy or religion, Rust is superior

For building a state-aware tree manipulation shell from scratch, Rust’s algebraic type system and memory model align directly with abstract syntax tree (AST) traversal and stateful REPL development.

Enum-Driven Tree Navigation: serde_json::Value mirrors nested tree structures naturally, making recursive node lookup and mutation fully type-safe without runtime type assertions or reflection.

Deterministic State Staging: Rust's ownership and borrowing model guarantees that isolated candidate configurations cannot leak into running states during concurrent validation or background schema checks.

Active REPL Infrastructure: Crates like reedline provide first-class support for dynamic, context-aware tab completion based on local AST nodes.

Single-Binary Distribution: Compiles down to a zero-dependency static binary with instant startup times, making it ideal for both human terminal workflows and rapid agentic tool invocations.

Related Idea - CLI as Code. That is, a small programmable kernel that can become any CLI - by configuring verbs, context aware nested structure, provider hooks etc, the kernel becomes a reusable substrate whenever any CLI tool is required for any project

A programmable CLI substrate decouples terminal mechanics (REPL, state navigation, path tracking, tab-completion) from business logic using a declarative configuration schema and dynamic extension hooks.

By treating the CLI shell as an extensible runtime, you can instantly turn any API, database, or domain model into a VyOS-style hierarchical shell without re-implementing line editing, history, or context tracking.

Core Architectural Primitives
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                   CLI Kernel Runtime                   │
│  (REPL, Navigation State, Candidates, Tab Completion)   │
└───────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────┘
                            │ Reads Grammar Spec
                            ▼
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                  Declarative Schema                    │
│      (Nodes, Validations, Custom Verbs, Help Specs)    │
└───────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────┘
                            │ Dispatches Events
                            ▼
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                   Provider Hooks                       │
│    [WASM Modules] | [IPC/gRPC] | [Embedded Scripting]  │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
1. The Declarative Schema (Grammar Specification)
Instead of writing imperative parser code, the user defines the CLI structure in a structured spec (such as KDL, TOML, or JSON). The kernel consumes this to dynamically construct the command tree and tab-completer at runtime.

Code snippet
// Example schema defining a database management CLI
node "database" help="Configure database connections" {
    node "cluster" help="Manage DB clusters" {
        node "node" pattern="[a-zA-Z0-9_-]+" help="Cluster node ID" {
            leaf "host" type="ip_or_hostname" help="Host IP or FQDN"
            leaf "port" type="integer" default=5432 help="Port number"
            leaf "role" type="enum" values="primary" "replica"
        }
    }
}
2. The State Substrate
The kernel maintains the interactive navigation runtime state:

Working Context: Tracks navigation pointers (edit, up, top).

Candidate State: Holds uncommitted tree modifications.

Running State: Reflects the active applied state.

Schema Engine: Validates types and required fields before candidate commits.

3. Provider Hooks (Execution Layer)
When the user executes commands like commit, show, set, or validate, the kernel delegates processing to external providers rather than hardcoding logic inside the shell binary.

WASM Plugins (Wasmtime / Wasmer): Sub-millisecond execution, completely sandboxed, and language-agnostic. Authors can write CLI logic in Rust, Go, Python, or Zig and compile to .wasm.

IPC / gRPC Plugins (HashiCorp Model): The CLI spawns child plugin binaries over standard pipes or domain sockets, enabling native execution and heavy system operations.

Embedded Scripting (Rhai / Lua): Lightweight inline scripts embedded directly inside the schema file for small data transformations and custom validations without extra compiled artifacts.

Standard Unix Pipelines: Dispatches execution down to simple stdin/stdout JSON payload transfers to executable scripts (hook.py, hook.sh).

Use Cases & Real-World Utility
Instant Admin Shells for APIs: Supply an OpenAPI/Swagger spec or GraphQL schema to the kernel, and it immediately boots an interactive REPL for inspecting and mutating remote infrastructure.

Universal Agent Execution Platform: Provides AI agents with a standardized, bounded interface (schema -> kernel -> tool hooks) for safely interacting with complex applications via token-efficient context trees.

Embedded Appliance Control: Provides hardware devices, routers, or containerized applications with unified configuration shells without rebuilding command-line parsers for each firmware update.

Unified Internal Tooling: Enterprise Platform Engineering teams can enforce consistent enterprise CLI interfaces across disparate backend services by distributing a single binary kernel paired with team-specific plugin schemas.

How does this idea interact with the previous one - JSON authoring - and are they separate tools that are independent, separate that are complementary, or some hybrid of the same tool?

They are best realized as a hybrid architecture: the JSON authoring tool is the core state engine of the programmable CLI kernel.

Every structured CLI config tree (VyOS, Cisco, Kubernetes) is ultimately a constrained JSON document. By building a single engine, you achieve a spectrum of utility based on whether a schema and hooks are attached.

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    Layer 2: Provider Hooks                  │
│       (WASM / gRPC / Shell scripts triggered on commit)     │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                 Layer 1: Schema & Constraints               │
│        (JSON Schema, KDL definitions, type validation)      │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│               Layer 0: Core JSON Authoring Engine           │
│     (Tree navigation, REPL, Candidate/Running state, Diffs) │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
Operating Modes of the Hybrid Architecture
1. Unconstrained Mode (Pure JSON Authoring)
When executed without a schema specification (clikernel edit document.json), the tool operates purely as Layer 0.

Any path can be edited (set a/b/c "value").

Tab completion auto-discovers keys from the current JSON document.

commit simply validates standard JSON syntax and flushes candidate state to disk.

2. Constrained Mode (Domain-Specific CLI)
When executed with a schema and hooks (clikernel --schema router.kdl --provider ./netlink.wasm), Layer 1 and Layer 2 activate over Layer 0.

Path Rules: Tab completion is locked to keys permitted by the schema.

Validation: Executing set port "abc" fails instantly because Layer 1 enforces integer types.

Semantic Verbs: The schema maps user-friendly CLI verbs to underlying JSON path operations (enable interface eth0 maps internally to set /interfaces/eth0/enabled true).

Execution: Executing commit compiles a JSON diff, passes it through Layer 1 validation, and forwards the payload to Layer 2 WASM/gRPC hooks to execute side effects.

Advantages of the Hybrid Approach
Single Codebase Maintainability: Shell mechanics—line editing, terminal history, context path manipulation (edit, up, top), tree diffing, and candidate-vs-running isolation—are written and tested once.

Agent Utility: AI agents use a single unified tool and interaction paradigm whether authoring unstructured JSON payloads or controlling domain-specific infrastructure.

Progressive Schema Adoption: Users can start with an unconstrained JSON document in Layer 0, gradually attach a JSON Schema in Layer 1 as system constraints solidify, and eventually attach Layer 2 WASM hooks to automate system execution.
